2026-01-13 11:33:38 - claude-primitive-finder - INFO - Claude thinks: Excellent! Now I can see:
- The ArrayBuffer was at `0x55cb385776a0` (1024 bytes)
- When resized to 8, the realloc returned the SAME address: `realloc(0x55cb385776a0, 8) = 0x55cb385776a0`

This means the realloc was done in-place (the chunk was not moved). In glibc, when you shrink a chunk significantly, the allocator might:
1. Return the same pointer (if the new size fits in the same bin or can be efficiently split)
2. Or reallocate to a new location (if beneficial)

In this case, the shrink from 1024 to 8 resulted in the same pointer being returned. This makes the vulnerability behavior dependent on whether:
